WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.810
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome back to the
program today, Congressman Ro Khanna, who

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.259
represents California's 17th District.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:11.950
Congressman, you know, I have to say, over
the last three months, there has sort of been

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:19.070
new life breathed into the Biden administration
and Joe Biden, a number of legislative accomplishments.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:25.560
He seems significantly more energetic, arguably
one of the most productive COVID isolation

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.849
periods of anyone I can think of.

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:31.210
Joe Biden had a couple of months ago.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:36.850
Do you get the sense that this will impact
November's midterms for Democrats in the House

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:43.280
like you, or that these are really siloed
accomplishments that that the Biden administration

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.280
is experiencing?

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.730
Speaker 4: There is a sense of momentum, and
that's because Democrats are delivering.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:52.150
The president delivered student debt relief.

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:56.360
We're bringing manufacturing back to America
with the semiconductor bill that I led with

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:57.900
chips and science.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:02.650
We are have the largest investment in climate
in American history.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:07.090
So there's a sense that what the president
said he would do, what we said we would do

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.030
in 2020, we are doing.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.159
And that is finally breaking through.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:16.280
And there's also a sense that the Democratic
Party is more unified than we've been probably

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.009
since the beginning of the president's term.

00:01:18.009 --> 00:01:21.200
Speaker 1: Yeah, that that seems absolutely
to be taking place.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.610
And when you particularly contrast to what's
happening in the Republican Party, where the

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:31.759
RNC is stopping paying Trump's legal bills,
and you're seeing significant disagreements

00:01:31.759 --> 00:01:37.249
on what the 2024 Republican nomination should
look like and whether the presence of people

00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:40.229
like Liz Cheney is even remotely welcome.

00:01:40.229 --> 00:01:45.429
It does seem like there is significantly more
unification among the Democratic Party.

00:01:45.429 --> 00:01:48.709
And also on some issues there seems to be
disagreement.

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:54.079
I mean, the student loan debt relief is an
example where you have some who believe, even

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.700
on the Democratic side, there should be no
such debt relief at all.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.149
People decided to take out these loans and
they should pay the loans.

00:02:01.149 --> 00:02:05.960
On the other hand, individuals who say all
student loan debt should be forgiven and then

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.710
folks kind of in the middle who say, well,
some some debt relief is a good idea.

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:12.840
Where are you on further debt relief?

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.620
Speaker 4: I'm for getting debt relief up
to 50,000.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:23.439
I mean, the reality is $10,000 is often what
it costs someone to do one semester at a college

00:02:23.439 --> 00:02:25.519
or one year at a community college.

00:02:25.519 --> 00:02:26.819
It's not enough.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:30.400
But I applaud the president for taking a major
step.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:33.730
This is going to help a lot of working and
middle class folks.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:36.569
It is, again, a precedent for doing more.

00:02:36.569 --> 00:02:40.030
So I want to applaud him but say that the
job is not done.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:43.490
Speaker 1: On the manufacturing jobs.

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:52.300
Explain to us exactly how one brings the job
back to the United States from overseas, where

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:58.610
that will increase the total cost to produce
whatever is being produced without making

00:02:58.610 --> 00:02:59.780
inflation worse.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.410
Like, what's the economic sequence wherein
we can do that?

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:08.409
Speaker 4: Actually producing more things
in the United States could help a long term

00:03:08.409 --> 00:03:09.409
with inflation.

00:03:09.409 --> 00:03:13.040
I mean, one of the reasons we had inflation
is we didn't make masks here.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.640
We didn't make semiconductors there.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:21.270
So you had to ship them from overseas at $15,000
shipping per container as opposed to $2,000.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:26.599
And the fact that we didn't have enough production
meant that we had too much money chasing too

00:03:26.599 --> 00:03:27.599
few goods.

00:03:27.599 --> 00:03:31.280
If you increase production long term, that
is deflationary.

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.609
But you're saying in a micro case, well, how
are you going to produce it here when wages

00:03:35.609 --> 00:03:38.400
may be higher, when environmental standards
may be higher?

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.849
I think that's why you need both government
collaboration in terms of financing.

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:47.930
I mean, other countries have helped finance
new factories, new industries, government

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:55.989
support on the workforce, development at community
colleges, and a collective regional economic

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:57.239
development effort.

00:03:57.239 --> 00:03:59.819
That's what we did with FDR to build our economy.

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:01.459
And there's no reason we can't do it now.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:06.540
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, I guess the shipping
is is certainly a factor, but 15,000 versus

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:14.650
two for a shipping container paying dramatically
lower wages on every unit on an ongoing basis

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:18.329
seems like it would be it would override the
shipping cost.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:21.880
I mean, I guess it depends on the product
and we'd have to sort of look at the details.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:26.789
But it seems also there probably has to be
some shift in the thinking from the American

00:04:26.789 --> 00:04:31.229
public about the advantages to domestic manufacturing
as well.

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:33.660
And I don't know that the messaging is really
there on that.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.449
Speaker 4: Well, I'm not sure it requires
a change in consumer behavior.

00:04:38.449 --> 00:04:43.180
Obviously, it'd be great to buy American,
but you could have government purchasing American

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.740
with all the purchasing power we have that
make a difference.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:52.120
Second, a lot of the advanced manufacturing
has much less labor costs.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.139
Because of automation, it has increased productivity.

00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:00.250
Now people say, well, this means less jobs,
which is true, but those are going to be higher

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.960
paying jobs and it's going to mean more competitiveness
for the product.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:10.150
So I think when it comes to it against manufacturing
interval, interval with textiles, for example,

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:15.180
as opposed to traditional manufacturing, that
can be done here and will be done here.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.100
And the quality is often higher here.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.570
That's not just an opinion.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:22.990
The productivity of American workers is more
than Germany or Japan.

00:05:22.990 --> 00:05:29.470
And so I think we have to be very deliberate
about the type of production that can be done.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:32.270
The government partnership that is required.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:34.960
And we can do it in a way that is cost competitive.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:35.960
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.410
You know, one one high tech product that is
being manufactured at least partially in the

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.349
United States is Tesla electric vehicles.

00:05:43.349 --> 00:05:47.289
And it's been very interesting to see that
many on the right.

00:05:47.289 --> 00:05:51.300
You would think, as I outlined yesterday,
they should love electric vehicles.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:56.200
You can charge them at home with solar panels
you own and not be.

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.200
Right.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.479
There's the self-sufficiency aspect to it.

00:05:59.479 --> 00:06:05.100
For people who like fast cars, which are associated
with alpha males in some right wing circles,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.880
electric vehicles are very fast.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.940
There's all these reasons why the right should
really be embracing electric vehicles, and

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.729
yet they seem almost angry about them to some
degree.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:20.500
Your colleague Marjorie Taylor GREENE and
many others are furious about electric vehicles.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.740
Are you surprised by the antipathy towards
them from some on the right?

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.830
Speaker 4: I'm not because that's horseshit.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:33.610
Climate has become so politicized that anything
that is almost good for the climate generates

00:06:33.610 --> 00:06:35.740
skepticism on the right.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:40.210
And that's why I think we could just talk
about the new industrialization of America.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:42.880
Let's build the new industries, new factories
here.

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:47.280
It turns out a lot of that will be clean and
a lot of that is going to be clean steel,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:52.979
new batteries, new electric vehicles, new
charging station, hydrogen jets, hydrogen

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:53.979
planes.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:58.699
We can talk about building new factories,
new industry, new manufacturing.

00:06:58.699 --> 00:07:03.620
And that may cut across the the the political
divide.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:07.000
Speaker 1: On on cannabis decriminalization.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:10.389
You and I have spoken about this before.

00:07:10.389 --> 00:07:16.849
My sense has been it's never been a particularly
interesting topic for President Joe Biden.

00:07:16.849 --> 00:07:20.480
But at the same time, I didn't think he was
going to do any student loan debt relief.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.770
And I was very, very much wrong there.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:29.860
Do you get any sense that there may be something
before November on cannabis from the Oval

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:30.860
Office?

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.210
Speaker 4: I don't you know, I obviously have
not just for decriminalizing it, I'm taking

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.970
for taking it off Schedule C, for legalizing
marijuana.

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:43.490
But I think this is something that's personal
for the president.

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:48.539
My understanding is that he has had people
with substance abuse in his own family, that

00:07:48.539 --> 00:07:49.900
that is informing his view.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:56.320
It's obviously a view that I disagree with,
but because it's something that seems to be

00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:02.610
a sincerely held conviction on his part, one
that I disagree with, I'm not sure that we're

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:03.660
going to see movement on that.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:09.259
Speaker 1: Do you do you expect that in the
long term, the cannabis issue is going to

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:13.970
be dealt with more at the state level as it
has been so far, or that at some point there

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:15.120
will be a federal change?

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.610
Speaker 4: I think it's going to start at
the state level.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:24.580
But my sense is within the next couple cycles,
presidential cycles, there'll be a federal

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:29.979
change because you have people on the Republican
side, the the younger generation that understands

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:36.539
the importance of legalizing cannabis and
the medical uses and the fact that cannabis

00:08:36.539 --> 00:08:42.190
often is less dangerous, certainly less dangerous
than the opioids, and yet more restrictive

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:43.870
than some of the opioids.

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:47.029
Speaker 1: Let's talk a little bit about the
upcoming midterms.

00:08:47.029 --> 00:08:52.540
So if we want to take just one forecast, the
FiveThirtyEight forecast says it's roughly

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:57.080
a 2 to 1 chance that Democrats hold the Senate.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:01.560
And by a roughly 4 to 1 margin, Democrats
are likely to lose the House.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.290
Now, on the House, some of the recent momentum
you mentioned is not really in those numbers

00:09:06.290 --> 00:09:10.450
quite yet, but it's a pretty significant hill
to climb at this point.

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:14.230
And sort of the most mathematically likely
outcome is a divided Congress.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:20.130
It seems to me that this will be two years
of total Republican obstruction to prevent

00:09:20.130 --> 00:09:28.100
any major legislation and then using the lack
of accomplishments that they cause as justification

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:30.970
to vote for a Republican president in 2024.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.700
That seems to be what the game plan is going
to be.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:38.240
Are you and your colleagues talking about
that as the most likely outcome and what what

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:39.709
can be done, if anything?

00:09:39.709 --> 00:09:44.690
Speaker 4: But we're still hopeful that we
can hold the house, especially after New York

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:45.690
19.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:50.140
And the surprising result there that Ryan
wasn't expected to win.

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:52.460
He really ran an abortion rate.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:59.120
We're seeing, as, you know, increased registration
among young women, in particular in Ohio,

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:00.459
in Pennsylvania.

00:10:00.459 --> 00:10:02.910
So are we underdogs to hold the house?

00:10:02.910 --> 00:10:03.910
Yes, of course.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:06.079
The president's party always loses seats.

00:10:06.079 --> 00:10:10.250
We're in a tough economic climate, but we're
still focused on how we do that.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:15.930
And the argument that we're making to it to
be able to do that is where is the Republican

00:10:15.930 --> 00:10:16.930
agenda?

00:10:16.930 --> 00:10:18.180
I mean, we have an agenda.

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.630
I can tell you what we will do on inflation.

00:10:20.630 --> 00:10:22.660
We're going to try to get childcare done.

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:28.670
We're going to make sure that we are providing
greater benefits for for Medicare.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.070
We're going to increase Social Security.

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:34.790
We're going to produce more things in America.

00:10:34.790 --> 00:10:37.300
The Republicans have no agenda.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:40.360
They're basically just running anti Biden.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.790
Speaker 1: Yeah, it's basically just obstruct.

00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:44.790
There's no doubt about that.

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:51.459
But nonetheless, if they successfully do that
for two years, it's not a super strong argument

00:10:51.459 --> 00:10:55.970
for Democrats to say, well, yes, we got nothing
done, but it wasn't our fault.

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:59.510
I mean, it just it doesn't sound like the
type of thing that voters will buy.

00:10:59.510 --> 00:11:02.110
So what do you do if that does end up being
the situation?

00:11:02.110 --> 00:11:04.090
Speaker 4: Well, it depends.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:09.660
I mean, obviously, President Obama and President
Clinton had obstructionist Republican Congresses

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:10.950
and both won reelection.

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:16.810
And they did it by showing just how extreme
the Republican Congresses work.

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:17.810
Right.

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:21.970
So it depends on whether the Republicans overplayed
their hand, which they're very capable of

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:22.970
doing.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:28.610
I mean, if all they're doing when they take
power is try to impeach Joe Biden and do hearings

00:11:28.610 --> 00:11:33.190
on Hunter Biden and all the other conspiracy
theories, then I think the American public

00:11:33.190 --> 00:11:36.910
will tire of that and say we need more reasonable
people.

00:11:36.910 --> 00:11:39.840
I in the past, but they've had the majority.

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:40.840
They have overreach.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.470
So there's no reason to think they wouldn't
do that again.

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:46.700
Speaker 1: Yeah, that laptop from hell is
really something.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:47.700
Congressman, we've got it.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:50.360
We've got to look at that hard drive, especially.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:51.880
Last thing I want to ask about.

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:57.800
How much in the among your colleagues is there
discussion of the Mar a Lago search warrant

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:03.060
and just the, you know, 60 truth social posts
a day from the former president?

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.019
Like something is very, very wrong over there.

00:12:06.019 --> 00:12:11.220
How much does that play in the day to day
between you and your colleagues and sort of

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:17.540
considering that side of what's taking place
in as you think about your 2022 campaign and

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:18.639
then moving beyond 22?

00:12:18.639 --> 00:12:22.220
Or is it wholly irrelevant to what you and
your colleagues are doing?

00:12:22.220 --> 00:12:27.980
Speaker 4: But we've tried not to talk about
it that much because it's a law enforcement

00:12:27.980 --> 00:12:30.779
matter and we've actually tried not to politicize
it.

00:12:30.779 --> 00:12:36.529
The last thing you want is every Democratic
member of Congress going on CNN or your podcast

00:12:36.529 --> 00:12:39.450
giving an opinion on what the Justice Department
should do.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:40.450
Right.

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:46.970
That said, what does come up are the threats,
frankly, that colleagues are facing in this

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:47.970
environment.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.050
And they're more than I've ever seen in six
years.

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:55.620
You had Eric Swalwell post yesterday about
the threat he received.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:12:59.000
One of his staffers received really ugly things.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:05.860
And unfortunately, I'm hearing that kind of
threats and violence much more from colleagues,

00:13:05.860 --> 00:13:06.860
which is concerning.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:11.470
Speaker 1: Are there I mean, obviously, I
don't want you to share what's being done,

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:17.910
but is there a different or more involved
sort of security apparatus that is that is

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.820
now involved because of this?

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:28.140
Speaker 4: There is, but you can't protect
in an open democracy, everyone from a culture

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:29.140
of violence.

00:13:29.140 --> 00:13:32.790
I mean, look, members of Congress, we are
in parades.

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:35.300
I was just in the pride parade in San Jose.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:38.100
We are we do town halls with hundreds of people.

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:40.009
We sit in coffee shops.

00:13:40.009 --> 00:13:42.100
And that's how it's supposed to be.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:49.509
And for many, many decades, people did that
to both parties without really worrying about

00:13:49.509 --> 00:13:50.509
political violence.

00:13:50.509 --> 00:13:55.730
I mean, obviously, you had crazy shootings
and mass shootings, but those were one off

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:56.730
incidents.

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:02.949
Now you're at a point where there are members
of Congress, unfortunately, who will be advised

00:14:02.949 --> 00:14:07.010
to sit out a town hall or a parade or a community
event.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:12.980
And this is one of the real damages that doesn't
get talked about enough in our democracy that

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:17.860
the right is causing, which is this fear of
being connected to people.

00:14:17.860 --> 00:14:18.860
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:14:18.860 --> 00:14:25.610
And sadly, the sort of a growing belief among
some that violence is an acceptable way to

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:30.730
try to achieve political change or to make
your opinions known, which is really scary.

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:32.960
And that goes to the gun culture aspect as
well.

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:33.960
Right?

00:14:33.960 --> 00:14:36.690
Seeing guns as a legitimate tool to solve
interpersonal problems.

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:41.660
Speaker 4: You know, when I was growing up,
the one thing almost a cliché was, well,

00:14:41.660 --> 00:14:44.329
in America, we solve our political problems
without violence.

00:14:44.329 --> 00:14:45.329
Right.

00:14:45.329 --> 00:14:46.329
Transfer of power.

00:14:46.329 --> 00:14:49.709
I mean, everyone would say that is a sort
of pointing to why we're the greatest democracy.

00:14:49.709 --> 00:14:56.500
And it's sad to me that something that basic
is now being being questioned.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:01.630
Fortunately, we still are in a relatively
peaceful place and these are threats and they

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:06.040
haven't become full for a full board assassination
attempts.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:12.680
But in other countries, you know, you literally
have political parties trying to assassinate

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:13.680
each other.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:20.170
And we've got to be very careful that we don't
cross into a line where violence becomes condoned

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:21.740
as part of the political culture.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:23.671
Speaker 1: Yeah, we don't want to we don't
